# ============================================================================
# P04 - POWER: trim unused cpufreq governors, pin zram writeback off
#                                        [EXPERIMENTAL / UNVERIFIED]
# ============================================================================
# WHAT IT CHANGES
#   CONFIG_CPU_FREQ_GOV_CONSERVATIVE=y -> not set
#   CONFIG_CPU_FREQ_GOV_POWERSAVE=y     -> not set
#   CONFIG_ZRAM_WRITEBACK               -> explicitly not set (already n)
#   CONFIG_CPU_FREQ_DEFAULT_GOV_SCHEDUTIL=y  (unchanged, stated for clarity)
#
#   baseline evidence (kernel source, commit 9b1c9a53):
#     k65v1_64_bsp_defconfig:48  CONFIG_CPU_FREQ_DEFAULT_GOV_SCHEDUTIL=y
#     k65v1_64_bsp_defconfig:49  CONFIG_CPU_FREQ_GOV_POWERSAVE=y
#     k65v1_64_bsp_defconfig:50  CONFIG_CPU_FREQ_GOV_CONSERVATIVE=y
#     k65v1_64_bsp_defconfig:108 CONFIG_MTK_CPU_FREQ=y
#     (oppo6765_defconfig:50-52 has the identical three governor lines)
#     drivers/block/zram/Kconfig:18  config ZRAM_WRITEBACK
#     drivers/block/zram/Kconfig:21          default n
#     k65v1_64_bsp_defconfig:83   CONFIG_ZRAM=y
#     init/Kconfig:258 config SWAP  ... default y   (so zram is usable)
#
# WHY IT SHOULD HELP ON A 4 GB MT6765V
#   1. Conservative exists because it deliberately ramps slowly to save power
#      on systems without good frequency control. On this platform the default
#      governor is already schedutil (CONFIG_CPU_FREQ_DEFAULT_GOV_SCHEDUTIL)
#      and CONFIG_MTK_CPU_FREQ is present, so conservative is not the tuning
#      path in use - it is a second, worse path that remains selectable.
#      The realistic risk is not "conservative is being selected" but that a
#      per-cluster or per-app scaling_governor write from a vendor HAL or
#      tuning service silently drops a cluster onto it. Removing the symbol
#      removes that failure mode AND removes governor code from vmlinux.
#   2. powersave pins a policy to its maximum frequency. It exists for
#      "battery life at any cost" use. It is not the Android default and
#      should not be selectable on a phone whose users expect responsiveness.
#   3. ZRAM_WRITEBACK is pinned off deliberately, not changed. Writeback
#      spills compressed pages to the backing store, which on a 64 GB eMMC
#      means extra writes and wear for no benefit on a device that already
#      has zram absorbing the anon-page pressure that causes reclaim. The
#      Kconfig default is already n; stating it means a future upstream
#      default flip cannot silently introduce flash writeback.
#
# WHAT WOULD VERIFY OR REFUTE IT
#   Before/after inventory - this is the primary check:
#     for p in /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpufreq/policy*; do
#         echo "$p: $(cat $p/scaling_governor) avail=[$(cat $p/scaling_available_governors)]"
#     done
#   Expect schedutil to remain the default and to be the only entry that
#   disappears from the available list. If any policy was NOT on schedutil
#   before the patch, STOP - that is a behaviour change you did not intend,
#   and the patch should be reverted.
#   Battery/idle: measure screen-off drain overnight, same brightness, same
#     charge, same network state, 3 nights each. See VALIDATION.md section 8.
#   Runtime frequency behaviour: sample scaling_cur_freq for all 8 policies
#     during an identical 5-minute workload; the frequency trace should be
#     unchanged (patch is a removal, not a retune).
#   Refutation: if overnight drain does not move AND vmlinux does not shrink,
#   the only remaining justification is removing an unintended escape hatch.
#   That may still be worth keeping, but say so honestly rather than
#   claiming a battery improvement.
#
# RISK
#   If any vendor HAL writes "powersave" or "conservative" to
#   scaling_governor, it will now fail with EINVAL. That failure is loud and
#   immediate (visible in logcat), not silent - but grep the target ROM's
#   init*.rc and vendor HALs for these strings before flashing.
# ============================================================================

# CONFIG_CPU_FREQ_GOV_CONSERVATIVE is not set
# CONFIG_CPU_FREQ_GOV_POWERSAVE is not set
CONFIG_CPU_FREQ_DEFAULT_GOV_SCHEDUTIL=y
# CONFIG_ZRAM_WRITEBACK is not set
